2563 0 obj
<<
/Title (Conclusion: in my measurements LBYL was faster at every bad-line rate \(1.5x at 0.1%, 3.6x at 50%\), partly because my EAFP version also pays for a function call per line. The key point is the trend: LBYL cost does not depend on how many lines are bad, while EAFP cost grows with every exception raised \(about 0.3 microseconds each\). So EAFP is acceptable when bad lines are rare and gives clearer code \(one place that describes the error\); when many lines are bad, explicit checks are clearly cheaper. For files, EAFP is also safer: checking os.path.exists\(\) first leaves a gap in which the file can disappear before open\(\). Space is)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2532 0 R
/Prev 2573 0 R
/Next 2550 0 R
>>
endobj
